der each render. \"Only call hooks from React functions\" — do not call hooks from plain JavaScript functions so that stateful logic stays with the component. Although these rules cannot be enforced at runtime, code analysis tools such as linters can be configured to detect many mistakes during development. The rules apply to both usage of Hooks and the implementation of custom Hooks,[27] which may call other Hooks. Server components[edit] React Server Components (RSC)[28] are function components that run exclusively on the server. The concept was first introduced in the talk \"Data Fetching with Server Components\".[29] Currently, server components are most readily usable with Next.js. With Next.js, it's possible to write components for both the server and the client (browser). When a server rendered component is received by the browser, React in the browser takes over and creates the virtual DOM and attaches event handlers. This is called hydration.[30] Though a similar concept to Server Side Rendering, RSCs do not send corresponding JavaScript to the client as no hydration occurs. As a result, they have no access to hooks. However, they may be asynchronous function, allowing them to directly perform asynchronous operations: async function MyComponent() { const message = await fetchMessageFromDb(); return ( <div>Message: {message}</div> ); } Class components[edit] Class components are declared using ES6 classes. The\n=== END PAGE TEXT ===\n\nGoal: Compare React and Vue.js based on their Wikipedia articles:\n\n1. Visit the React (software) article on Wikipedia\n2. Record react_creator (key=react_creator) and react_release_year (key=react_release_year) in working memory\n3. Navigate to the Vue.js article on Wikipedia\n4. Record vue_creator (key=vue_creator) and vue_release_year (key=vue_release_year) in working memory\n5. Call verify_completion for the goal_complete criterion after all 4 facts are recorded\n6. Call terminate(finalAnswer=...) with a 2-3 sentence comp